<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miraj</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miraj is a town in Sangli district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> river. Its fort was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, and in the 18th century it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patwardhan">Patwardhan family</a>, generals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a>. Sangli broke away from it in 1782, and the remaining lands were later divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of Miraj Senior and Miraj Junior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miraj.jpg" alt="Miraj" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekmirajkar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirajmarketnorth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Miraj is part of the Sangli-Miraj-Kupwad metropolitan region in the Sangli district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. Marathi is the official language, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> is also widely spoken and a form of Hindustani, Hyderabadi Deccani, is heard as well. The town grew into a vital junction of the railway network, and Miraj Junction was once the only junction to carry all three rail gauges: broad, narrow and metre gauge. The last narrow gauge train left on 1 November 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the end of the 9th century the Shilaharas of Kolhapur gained control of Miraj, and inscriptions describe their ruler Gonka as the conqueror of Karahata (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karad">Karad</a>), Miraj and Konkan. In 1216 the town passed with other Shilahara lands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas of Devagiri</a>. One account relates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah">Hasan Gangu</a>, founder of the Bahmani dynasty, raised an army with a treasure he found, defeated Rani Durgavati, the subedar of Miraj, and took its fort; in 1347 the town was renamed Mubarakabad. In 1490 it passed to the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-i">Ibrahim Adil Shah I</a> kept his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a> under house arrest there.</p><p>On 28 November 1659, soon after the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> general Afzal Khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a>, the western Adil Sahi district was surrendered to Annaji Datto, finance minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, but the fort of Miraj held out. Netaji Palkar besieged it and Shivaji came to command the siege in January 1660, then abandoned it when attacks by Siddi Johar and Fazal Khan drew him back to Panhala. Shahu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> captured the town on 3 October 1739 after a campaign of two years, and in 1761 Gopalrao, son of Harbhat Patwardhan, took the Miraj jagir from Peshwa Madhavrao. In 1820 the state was split into Miraj Senior and Miraj Junior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Miraj Senior acceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 8 March 1948, and the town has been part of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> since 1960. It supplies Indian string instruments such as the sitar, sarod and tanpura, a craft developed by Faridsaheb Shikalgar in the 18th century, whose descendants took the surname Sitarmaker. The singer Abdul Karim Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirana-gharana">Kirana gharana</a> lies buried at a dargah in the town, where an annual music festival is held in his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miraj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Miraj</b> Junior was created in 1820 when the Patwardhan holding of Miraj, itself one of the shares from the family partition of 1811, was divided between two branches.</blockquote><a href="/state/miraj-junior">Miraj Junior</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miraj</b> had been the seat of the Patwardhan family, Chitpavan Brahmin generals of the Peshwas.</blockquote><a href="/state/miraj-senior">Miraj Senior</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1782 Chintamanrao Patwardhan, known as Appa Saheb, separated from the family seat at <b>Miraj</b> and made Sangli his own state, building its fort and the Ganpati temple.</blockquote><a href="/state/sangli-state">Sangli State</a></li></ul></section>
